course: dictionary
lesson: 2262
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word cream has five meanings, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Look at the words around it. «Sun cream should be at least SPF 30 with good UVA protection, put on before going out.» Here cream is something soft you rub on your skin, because sun cream protects you from the sun.
explain: Cream can also be food, as in whipped cream on strawberries, or a colour. Try each meaning in the sentence and see which one still makes sense. Cream as a colour fits «a cream sofa» but not «sun cream».
explain: Some uses are about the everyday world, like «numbing cream so the needle hurts less», where cream is a soft medicine you rub on, not something to eat. When a word has more than one meaning, the context is your best clue.
fact: Cream is the thick, fatty part of milk, used to make butter, cakes and ice cream.
fact: A cream is a soft, thick stuff you rub on your skin, like sun cream.
fact: Cream is also a pale yellow-white colour, so a cream-coloured wall is not quite white.
fact: To cream butter and sugar means to mix them until they are soft.
fact: The cream of something is the very best part of it, as in «the cream of the crop».
exercise: «Sun cream should be put on before going out in the sun.» What kind of cream is sun cream? | A pale yellow-white colour | To mix until soft | *A soft, thick stuff you rub on your skin | A thick fatty part of milk
exercise: Connor says a double-double is «coffee with two creams and two sugars». What is a double-double? | *Coffee with two creams and two sugars | A cake with two layers | A type of sun cream | A woolly hat
exercise: «She is the cream of the crop at chess.» What does cream of the crop mean? | Lots of milk | The worst one in the group | A crop of cream from the farm | *The very best
test: «Whip the cream until it is thick, then spoon it onto the strawberries.» What is the cream in this sentence? | A soft stuff you rub on your skin | *The thick fatty part of milk, used in desserts | A pale yellow colour | A kind of ice cube
test: «Cream the butter and sugar until they are soft.» What does cream mean as a verb here? | To turn pale yellow | To count out scoops | *To mix until soft | To rub on skin
exam: «The cream walls of the hall were lovely and warm.» What does cream describe here? | A thick part of milk | Mixing until soft | A soft thing for skin | *A pale yellow-white colour
